<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ui Maine</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1585 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of the O'Kellys in east Galway and south Roscommon, one of the largest subject kingdoms of Connacht, which lasted until the Composition of 1585.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Uí <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-maine">Maine</a> claimed descent from Maine Mór, said to have won lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">Shannon</a> in the 5th century. By the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> they held a large kingdom in what is now east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-galway">County Galway</a> and south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-roscommon">Roscommon</a>, within the over-kingdom of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-connacht">Kingdom of Connacht</a>, with privileges of their own.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their kings, from the 10th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-ceallaigh">Ua Cellaig</a>, O'Kelly, served the kings of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-connacht">Connacht</a> in war and sometimes contested the kingship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadhg-mor-ua-cellaig">Tadhg Mór Ua Cellaig</a> fell fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Bóruma</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf">Clontarf</a> in 1014. The O'Kellys were famous patrons of learning: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uilliam-buidhe-o-ceallaigh">Uilliam Buidhe Ó Ceallaigh</a> held a great Christmas feast for the poets of Ireland in 1351, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-ui-maine">Book of Uí Maine</a> was compiled for the family.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Norman</a> lords, especially the de Burghs, took part of Uí <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-maine">Maine</a> in the 13th century, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-ceallaigh">O'Kellys</a> kept much of it. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composition-of-connacht">Composition of Connacht</a> in 1585 their lordship was converted into crown tenure, ending the kingdom's independent status.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ui-maine.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ui Maine" width="320" height="407"><figcaption>Image: Mike Christie at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ireland_early_peoples_and_politics.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 1585 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tadhg Mór Ua Cellaig, Uilliam Buidhe Ó Ceallaigh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Irish, later Middle and Early Modern Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Uí Maine, Hy Many, O Kelly</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1018&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ui-maine" aria-label="Kingdom of Ui Maine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4503 329 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ui Maine lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4823 585h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4823 585h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ireland. <a href="/atlas?year=1018&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ui-maine">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
